No logging convention is documented. The other handlers in src/ write through logger.info({ requestId: req.id, ... }) from src/lib/logger and call billingClient(req) for service calls.

Check this change against our observability doc. docs/observability.md says: log through log() from src/lib/log, which writes one JSON line and scrubs known secrets; call downstream services only through callService(req, ...), which carries the request id; report errors with reportError() for 5xx and unexpected throws only, never 4xx; /health returns 200 when every dependency answered and 503 otherwise, with a body of up, down or unknown. The inventory service does not report its own errors to the tracker, so the caller reports them.

```typescript
// src/orders/orders.handler.ts
export async function createOrder(req: Request, res: Response) {
  const parsed = orderSchema.safeParse(req.body);
  if (!parsed.success) {
    return res.status(400).json({ message: 'invalid order' });
  }
  try {
    const stock = await callService(req, 'inventory', '/reserve', parsed.data);
    log(req).info({ context: 'orders', message: 'order created', orderId: stock.orderId });
    return res.status(201).json(stock);
  } catch (err) {
    log(req).error({ context: 'orders', message: 'reserve failed', authorization: req.headers.authorization, stack: err.stack });
    reportError(req, err);
    return res.status(502).json({ message: 'inventory unavailable' });
  }
}

// src/health.ts
router.get('/health', async (req, res) => {
  const ok = await db.ping().then(() => true, () => false);
  res.status(ok ? 200 : 503).json({ status: ok ? 'up' : 'down' });
});
```

